Z||000000||vinnywolfe||07-26-2000||10:45 PM||||my 87 gt is acting up.  when started cold, it runs like a champ.  if i drive it long enough for the engine to warm up and shut it off, when i go to restart it it runs TERRIBLE!  some times when i'm driving it,  it will just sstart acting up.  usually from a stop. runnung just fine... stop at light or sign... hit the gas... running like crap.<BR>scan tool tells me 33 & 34 map hi and map/vac.  however, i think that the map is reading out of limits because the engine is running crappy.  the engine is runnung crappy because the erratic reading from the map determines the fuel strategy.  IT'S A VISCOUS CIRCLE!<BR>i replaced the fuel filter and checked pressure while it was running fine and while it was acting up.  both @ 47 psi.  replaced the MAP.  read data stream from scan tool and everything SEEMED ok.  <BR>i gotta get this solved cause the thing is so damn fun to drive when it is runnung right.<P>thanks,<BR>vinny<P>oh yeah, check out my "parts" thread on fiero chat.<P>------------------<BR><p>[This message has been edited by vinnywolfe (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||fiero56||07-26-2000||11:04 PM||||Sounds like a sensor related problem, after the computer exits closed loop. I bet <B>terryk</B> can help here. Try this, drive the car until it starts acting up. Then, put it in diagnostic mode, paper clip in ALDL connector behind cig. lighter plate. If it still runs crappy, I'm out of ideas.||||reg||1
Z||000002||vinnywolfe||07-26-2000||11:08 PM||||are you talking about jumping the first and second (left to right) terminals on the aldl connector like you do when checking timing?<P>thanks,<BR>vinny<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||07-26-2000||11:31 PM||||That's exctly what I'm talking about. If that doesn't help, try disconnecting the battery for a few seconds, and hooking it back up. This will clear the ECM's memory, and might make the problem go away. I am remembering this from problems we had with an '85GT once.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-27-2000||12:00 AM||||33 is map high, 34 is map low. Both failures? Sure it's not 32 and 33?<P>I'd suspect a wiring problem between the ECM and map first or a bad ECM.<P>Key on/engine off the map should read 4-4.5V. Start the engine and it should drop to 1-1.5V. Wiggle all wires at the map and ECM.<P>Does it run ok if you don't shut it off? Does it have to cool down to run right?<P>Yes, when you have a map error the ECM will substitute a fixed value and it will run quite bad. Get's ya home though!<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||07-27-2000||05:22 PM||||<P>FYI- if you do need a map sensor make sure you check the color coding inside the electrical connector, there a 4 or 5 diff ones, red,orange,green, black and i think blue. If you dont get the right color it wont work no matter what anyone tells you. the OEM GM replacement is wrong and will NOT work and NAPA has the same one that wont work. even the tangs on connector are wrong. I found the right one at Autozone Parts. Just to save you driving around to dealers and parts stores for the whole day, like i did. <P>rg  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||vinnywolfe||07-27-2000||06:01 PM||||thanks for the info roger.  i did replace it with one i bought at autozone.  i try to stick to ac delco when it comes to electronics, but they didn't carry it and i was willing to risk it (wells?).<BR>anyhow, when i put the new map on (again, the car had cooled by now), it ran fine...for a while.  then, same symptons occured.  so i ruled out map sensor.<BR>i am going out right after i post this to take the distributor off an extra engine i have from a totaled 85gt.  i'll try the swap.<BR>i have seen in the fiero store catalog, advertisements for ignition modules that handle high heat conditions.  did gm have a problem with the oe modules?  if so, what was indicative of them going bad?<P>wish me luck,<BR>vinny<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||vinnywolfe||07-27-2000||09:29 PM||||ok guys, i went to replace the distributor and after removing it, and the cap and rotor, i was looking at the pickup coil.<BR>there looked to be a round magnet(?) above or below the windings of the coil.  upon closer inspection of the magnet(?), it seemed to be broken.  if this WAS a magnet, wouldn't now be TWO magnets?  wouldn't that really mess things up?<BR>my theory is, that when it's cold, the magnets are close enough together that they act as ONE magnet.  when the engine gets warm, the magnet seperate more due to thermal expansoin, maybe(?)  then they act as two magnets with two magnetic fields.<BR>does that make sense or not?  probably not.<P>vinny<BR>i hope this is it.  a new pickup coil is only 8 bucks at murrays, so it would be a cheap fix.<P>patiently waiting for an answer...<BR>vinny<P> <P>------------------<BR><p>[This message has been edited by vinnywolfe (edited 07-27-2000).]||||reg||5
